dzn: Work around two blending bugs of Intel's Direct3D 12 driver

Intel's driver for a UHD 770 under WSL2 (32.0.101.7092) ignores the blend
factor of OMSetBlendFactor: it blends as if each colour channel of the
constant were rounded to 0 or 1 and its alpha were 0, so every
glBlendColor blend of an OpenGL ES app came out wrong (1513 dEQP-GLES2 and
GLES3 fragment_ops.blend failures; dEQP-VK.dynamic_state cb_state, and
about half of the dEQP-VK.pipeline blend states with constants, on every
format). The driver of an Iris Plus 650 (31.0.101.2145) blends the
constants correctly.

On Intel, a pipeline that blends one colour attachment with constant
factors, without a logic op or dual-source factors of its own, takes the
constants as dual-source (SRC1) factors instead: its fragment shader
writes the blend constants, from a new blend_constants field of the
runtime data, as its second output.

The same driver writes nothing for a dual-source blend whose alpha factors
take SRC1 when the blend does not write alpha: alpha is out of the write
mask, or the format has none (R8G8, R16, R5G6B5 and the like). On Intel,
alpha factors of a blend that does not write alpha are ONE and ZERO, and
they do not count when deciding on the constants above.

A fragment shader that already writes a second output the blend does not
use gets the constants in that output.

dEQP-VK.pipeline.monolithic.blend on a UHD 770: 1151 failures, down from
4481 (r8g8b8a8_unorm states: 100 of 100, up from 39); none new on an RTX
4080. Constant blending on more than one colour attachment stays wrong on
the UHD 770, as Direct3D 12 blends with two sources only into one.

